  "description": "Quantize flat weight vectors in contiguous groups to signed INT4 values with per-group scales.\n\nGroup-wise quantization confines outlier values to individual blocks, preserving LLM attention and MLP layer quality.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\ns_g = \\frac{\\max |w_g|}{7}, \\quad q_i = \\operatorname{clip}\\left(\\operatorname{round}\\left(\\frac{w_i}{s_g}\\right), -8, 7\\right)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Partition flat weights into groups of size G\n2. Compute scale factor s_g = max(|w|)/7 for each group\n3. Clamp quantized values to [-8, 7] and dequantize\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'quantized', 'scales', and 'dequantized'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Implement Group-Wise INT4 Quantization\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nRunning large AI models in production requires massive computing power. During text generation, memory bandwidth (fetching weights and past key-value caches from VRAM) is often the primary bottleneck rather than raw arithmetic compute. **Implement Group-Wise INT4 Quantization** optimizes hardware efficiency, memory consumption, or request serving throughput.\n\nGroup-wise quantization confines outlier values to individual blocks, preserving LLM attention and MLP layer quality. In this problem, the goal is to: quantize flat weight vectors in contiguous groups to signed int4 values with per-group scales.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\ns_g = \\frac{\\max |w_g|}{7}, \\quad q_i = \\operatorname{clip}\\left(\\operatorname{round}\\left(\\frac{w_i}{s_g}\\right), -8, 7\\right)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Partition flat weights into groups of size G\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute scale factor s_g = max(|w|)/7 for each group\n3. **Phase 3**: Clamp quantized values to [-8, 7] and dequantize\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Memory Bandwidth Bottleneck**: Quantizing weights to INT8 or INT4 cuts memory traffic in half or quarters, dramatically increasing token generation speeds.\n* **Serving Systems**: Dynamic batching and PagedAttention maximize GPU utilization by grouping requests arriving at different times.\n* **Latency vs. Throughput**: In production serving, systems engineers balance Time-To-First-Token (TTFT) against overall tokens-per-second throughput.",
