  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Implement Group-Wise INT4 Quantization** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef inference_groupwise_int4_quantization(weights: list, group_size: int = 4) -> dict:\n    w = np.array(weights, dtype=np.float64).flatten()\n    gs = int(group_size)\n    num_groups = int(np.ceil(len(w) / gs))\n    scales, quantized, dequant = [], [], []\n    for g in range(num_groups):\n        grp = w[g*gs : (g+1)*gs]\n        sc = max(float(np.max(np.abs(grp)) / 7.0), 1e-8)\n        q = np.clip(np.round(grp / sc), -8, 7).astype(int)\n        scales.append(round(sc, 6))\n        quantized.extend(q.tolist())\n        dequant.extend(np.round(q * sc, 6).tolist())\n    return {'quantized': quantized, 'scales': scales, 'dequantized': dequant}\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
